. /*------------------------------------------------------------------------
> 
> NO TRIMMING -- AND THE REASON RE-RUNS ITSELF.
> 
> A per-capita outcome in levels is the one place in this table where a
> handful of county-years can move a coefficient, and the log outcome in
> Table 1 compresses that by construction. So the tail was tested rather than
> assumed. That test used to live in this comment; it now runs, because a
> number in a comment is a claim about a vintage that may no longer exist.
> 
> Panel D, column 4 specification, three samples:
> 
>     full sample                            -0.3550  (0.0707)
>     
>     less the top 1% of county-years        -0.2244  (0.0539)
>     
>     less the top 5% of counties by mean    -0.1511  (0.0422)
> 
> [VERIFIED 09/17] All three reproduce off the current panel to four
> decimals. The magnitude falls by a third and then by more than half, but
> the sign and the 1% significance hold throughout -- which is why the table
> is untrimmed, and why what the block below tests is the SIGN and the
> significance, not the magnitudes. The magnitudes are allowed to move with
> the vintage; the qualitative claim is not.
> 
> Nothing here feeds the table: two extra reghdfe calls, reported and thrown
> away.
> 
> The tails themselves are listed after, so the expectation behind the
> decision is checkable rather than asserted in prose: the counties at the
> top should be rural, high-harvest and small, and the counties at the
> bottom should be independent cities and urban counties. On the 09/17/2026
> vintage they are -- Highland VA (4,698 head on 2,607 residents in 1992,
> 1,802 per 1,000) and Florence WI at the top; Orange CA, Sacramento CA and
> Milwaukee WI at the bottom of the positive rates; and the zeros are San
> Francisco, Menominee, and a run of Virginia independent cities.
> 
> ------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
